# monomorph: generic method in generic type — per-method-instantiation stamping
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The 4th call shape — a GENERIC method on a GENERIC type (`Gen<T>::g2<T2>()` and the inferred
# `Gen<T>::g2(x)`) — did not lower: codegen emitted a dangling `tk_t_T2`. The parser + checker already
# resolve BOTH positions (`owner_type_args` binds the type `T`, `callee_type_args` binds the method
# `T2`); the hole was purely in the MONOMORPH. `stamp_one_instance_method` carimba a single concrete copy
# of each instance method with `type_params = []`, which ERASES the method-level `T2`, so the stamped body
# kept `T2` unresolved. (The same wall hit the INFERRED form on the pre-feature compiler — the feature
# only EXPOSED the hole, it did not create it.)
# The cure composes the TWO substitutions and stamps the generic method PER METHOD-INSTANTIATION, into the
# concrete instance's namespace — the method-level twin of how free generic fns are stamped:
#   - `stamp_instance_methods` now SKIPS a method that carries method-level type-params (the erasing
#     single-copy stamp is exactly what emitted `tk_t_T2`); a NON-generic method of a generic type stamps
#     unchanged (byte-identical — the whole existing corpus).
#   - `mono_texpr` (the call-site walk) detects a generic method of a generic-type INSTANCE
#     (`is_generic_instance_method_call`: `gf` is generic AND `call_ns` is a `__g__` instance whose base
#     owns `gf`) and composes the INSTANCE Subst (owner `T → concrete` + the `Base → Gen__g__i64` receiver
#     remap, from `instance_method_subst_l5`) with the METHOD Subst (`T2 → concrete`, from
#     `mono_call_subst`) into one `compose_method_subst`. It records a `MonoInst` carrying the new
#     `owner_ns` field = the instance namespace.
#   - PHASE-2 stamping lands the method in `owner_ns` (`Gen__g__i64::g2__g__i64`), not the abstract
#     template's namespace, so the symbol matches the call site's `call_ns`. The dedup ledger
#     (`mono_inst_seen`) keys on (owner_ns × mangle), so `Gen<i64>::g2<i64>` and `Gen<str>::g2<i64>` stamp
#     as two distinct symbols instead of colliding on the bare method mangle `g2__g__i64`.
#   - `mono_call_subst` gained an `owner_tps` parameter: for the INFERRED generic-instance call the
#     desugared receiver param is typed as the owner generic (`Gen<T>`/`Self`), which the method's own
#     type-param scope cannot resolve; with the owner params in scope the receiver pattern resolves and a
#     param that binds no method type-param is skipped. Empty `owner_tps` = the pre-existing free-fn path,
#     byte-identical.
# The arity cap (`max_generic_arity()` = 16) is untouched and still gates both positions.
# NON-REGRESSION (gated additive): `Tipo<T>::func()` (gen type, non-gen method — `Lst<i64>::make`),
# `Tipo::func<T>()` (non-gen type, gen method — `Plain::f1<i64>`), instance `x.m<T>()` on a non-gen type
# (`Box::wrap<i64>`), and every NON-generic method of a generic type all stay on their existing paths —
# `is_generic_instance_method_call` returns false and every added branch collapses to the old behavior. A
# generic type with only non-generic methods emits byte-identical C old-vs-new (proven on a `Cell<T>` with
# a plain `get()`).
# Proven by examples/probes/explicit_type_args, extended with the 4th shape: `Gen<i64>::g2<i64>(7)`, the
# inferred `Gen<i64>::g2(8)`, the `T != T2` `Gen<str>::g2<i64>(9)`, and an instance `gi.pick<str>(...)` /
